var e=1,t=`funds-sideletters-01`,n=`fund-structures`,r=`Side Letters, MFN and Co-Investment`,i=`Not every investor in a fund has the same deal — and the mechanisms that decide whether that matters to you.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`funds-gplp-01`],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`Two investors commit to the same fund on the same day. They do not necessarily have the same terms.

A **[[side-letter]]** is a bilateral agreement between the GP and one investor, modifying the partnership agreement for that investor alone. It is entirely standard, and the things it typically contains are:

**Fee discounts.** Reduced [[management-fee|management fee]] or [[carried-interest|carry]], usually for large or early [[commitment|commitments]].

**Enhanced reporting.** More frequent or more detailed information than other investors receive.

**Excuse rights.** The ability to be excluded from investments that breach the investor's own policies — a pension fund's exclusions, a sovereign entity's restrictions.

**[[co-investment|Co-investment]] rights.** Access to invest directly alongside the fund in individual deals, usually at reduced or no fees.

**Advisory committee seats.** A place where conflicts and valuation questions get discussed.

**Regulatory accommodations.** Provisions a particular investor needs to comply with its own rules.

The practical consequence: **the document you signed is not the whole agreement governing the fund you are in.**`},{type:`intuition`,id:`why-mfn-matters`,body:`If other investors can negotiate better terms privately, how do you avoid being the one who did not?

The answer is a **[[most-favoured-nation]] clause** — an MFN. It gives you the right to see other side letters and elect any provision you prefer. In effect it converts every other investor's negotiation into an option for you.

But MFNs are almost always **tiered by commitment size**, and this is the part worth understanding. A typical structure lets you elect provisions granted to investors who committed the same amount or less. So a $200m investor can take a $150m investor's terms but not a $500m investor's.

That is defensible — larger cheques buying better terms is ordinary commercial practice. It also means **the value of your MFN depends entirely on where your commitment sits in the distribution**, and an MFN with a tier just above your commitment size can be worth very little.

The genuinely important mechanical point: **an MFN is worthless if you do not exercise it.** The process is usually a bundle of redacted side letters delivered at closing with a short election window. Reviewing them is real work, and an investor who does not do that work has paid for an option and let it expire. **That happens more often than it should.**`},{type:`concept`,id:`co-investment`,body:`[[co-investment|Co-investment]] deserves separate treatment, because it is the most economically significant thing a side letter typically grants.

The arrangement: the GP finds a deal too large for the fund alone, or wants to reduce concentration, and offers LPs the chance to invest directly in that specific company — usually at **reduced or zero fees and carry**.

The appeal is obvious. Deploying capital at no fee alongside a manager you have already diligenced materially improves blended returns.

The complications are less obvious and matter:

**Adverse selection.** What gets offered as co-investment? If a GP offers its best deals to the fund and its marginal ones to co-investors, the co-investment portfolio underperforms the fund. This can also run the other way, so the question is factual rather than presumed: **compare the historical performance of co-investments against the main funds.**

**Speed.** Co-investment decisions typically need making in one to three weeks. An investor without the internal capability to underwrite that fast either declines — losing the benefit — or accepts without proper work, which is worse.

**Concentration.** Co-investing means a much larger position in a single company than the fund's diversification would give you. The fee saving is real and so is the concentration.

**Allocation fairness.** Where several LPs want the same co-investment, how is it allocated? A written policy is the answer you want.`},{type:`table`,id:`what-to-ask-for`,caption:`Side letter provisions, by value to the investor`,headers:[`Provision`,`Value`,`Note`],rows:[[`[[co-investment|Co-investment]] rights`,`Highest`,`No-fee deployment alongside a diligenced manager. Requires internal capability to use.`],[`[[most-favoured-nation|MFN]] election right`,`High`,`Only if the tier is at or below your commitment size, and only if you actually review the letters.`],[`Fee discount`,`High and simple`,`Directly quantifiable. Usually the first thing asked for and the most contested.`],[`Advisory committee seat`,`Moderate`,`Visibility into conflicts and valuations rather than control.`],[`Enhanced reporting`,`Moderate`,`Useful if you have the capacity to act on it; otherwise decorative.`],[`Excuse rights`,`Situational`,`Essential if you have policy exclusions, otherwise irrelevant.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Not exercising the MFN.** The most common and most avoidable failure. A bundle of redacted letters arrives with a two-week election window and nobody reads them properly. The option expires unused.

**An MFN tier that excludes everything useful.** If the meaningful concessions went to investors above your tier, your MFN grants you access to provisions you did not want.

**Co-investment adverse selection.** Being shown the deals the GP is less certain about. Detectable by comparing historical co-investment performance with fund performance, which is a fair thing to request.

**Co-investing without capability.** Accepting a three-week deadline on a concentrated single-company position without the ability to underwrite it properly. The fee saving does not compensate for skipping diligence.

**Preferential liquidity granted to others.** The most serious version in open-ended funds: if another investor has better redemption terms via a side letter, they can exit ahead of you in stress. **This is worth specifically asking about** and is a reasonable thing to require an MFN over.

**Undisclosed side letters.** Where the GP is not obliged to disclose them at all, you have no way to know what others were granted. That absence is itself a term worth negotiating.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`liquidity-side-letters`,body:`**In an open-ended fund, a side letter granting another investor better liquidity is not a fee issue — it is a queue position.**

A fee discount to a larger investor costs you nothing directly. Preferential redemption rights cost you a great deal in exactly the scenario that matters: they let that investor exit before the [[gate]] binds, funded by the sale of the best assets, leaving you with the residue.

So the questions to ask are specific. Does any investor have shorter notice, a shorter [[lockup|lock-up]], or an exemption from the gate? Is the GP obliged to tell me? And does my MFN cover liquidity provisions, or only economic ones — because **many MFNs explicitly carve out liquidity terms**, which is the single most important exclusion to check for.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Read the side letters when the MFN bundle arrives.** Diarise the election deadline the day you sign. This is the single highest-value hour in the whole process and it is routinely skipped.

**Check what your MFN tier actually is, and estimate who sits above it.** If most of the fund's capital came from investors larger than you, the MFN may grant little.

**Ask explicitly whether any investor has preferential liquidity terms.** Then ask whether your MFN covers liquidity provisions. The two answers together tell you your real queue position.

**For co-investment, ask for the historical performance of co-investments versus the funds.** A GP that has tracked it and will share it is answering the adverse selection question with data.

**Ask for the written co-investment allocation policy.** Not the intention — the policy.

**Be honest about whether you can use co-investment rights.** Rights you cannot exercise in three weeks are worth nothing, and negotiating for them instead of a fee discount is a poor trade.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Side letters mean the partnership agreement is not the whole agreement. Other investors in your fund may have materially different terms.`,`An MFN converts others' negotiations into an option for you — but it is tiered by commitment size and worthless if you do not review the letters and elect.`,`Co-investment is usually the most valuable provision available, and it carries adverse selection, speed and concentration risks that need testing with data.`,`Preferential liquidity granted to another investor is a queue position, not a fee. Check whether your MFN covers liquidity terms, because many explicitly do not.`]}],u=[{id:`funds-sideletters-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`side-letter`,`structure`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`What is a side letter?`,choices:[`A summary of the partnership agreement provided to prospective investors`,`A bilateral agreement between the GP and one investor that modifies the partnership terms for that investor alone`,`A quarterly reporting document sent to all limited partners`,`A legal notice required when a fund changes its investment strategy`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`That describes a term sheet or private placement memorandum summary, which applies to everyone.`,`Correct. It is a private variation for one investor, which is why the document you signed does not fully describe the fund you are in.`,`Reporting is governed by the partnership agreement, and enhanced reporting can itself be a side letter provision.`,`Strategy changes require consents under the agreement; that is a different mechanism.`],explanation:`Side letters are entirely standard and not improper. The consequence to internalise is that terms in a fund are investor-specific, which is what makes MFN rights valuable.`},{id:`funds-sideletters-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`mfn`,`side-letter`,`tiering`],concept:`why-mfn-matters`,stem:`You commit $200m and have an MFN tiered at your commitment level. Most of the fund's capital came from investors committing $500m or more. What follows?`,choices:[`You can elect any provision granted to any investor`,`Your MFN is of limited value, since the meaningful concessions likely went to investors above your tier`,`The MFN is void because of the tiering`,`You are entitled to the same fee as the largest investor`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Tiering is the whole point: you can elect provisions granted to investors at or below your size, not above it.`,`Correct. An MFN is only as good as what was granted within your tier. Where the capital and the concessions sit above you, the right grants access to terms you likely already have.`,`Tiering is standard and does not void anything. It defines the scope.`,`That is precisely what tiering prevents. Larger commitments buying better terms is ordinary practice.`],explanation:`The practical step is to estimate the commitment distribution before valuing the MFN. If you are at the small end of a fund dominated by very large investors, the right is worth less than it sounds — and a direct fee negotiation may be the better use of your leverage.`},{id:`funds-sideletters-01-q3`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:3,tags:[`mfn`,`process`,`misconception`],concept:`why-mfn-matters`,stem:`"We have an MFN, so we are protected against other investors getting better terms." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — an MFN is an option that must be actively exercised within a short window after reviewing the other letters`,`False — because MFN clauses are unenforceable in practice`,`True — an MFN automatically confers any better terms granted to others`,`False — because MFNs only apply to funds above a certain size`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. The mechanism is a bundle of redacted side letters delivered at closing with a limited election period. No review, no election, no benefit — and the option expires quietly.`,`MFNs are generally enforceable. The failure is procedural, not legal.`,`Nothing is automatic. This is the misconception, and it is the reason MFNs are frequently paid for and unused.`,`There is no such size threshold.`],explanation:`This is a rare case where a real protection is routinely lost to administration rather than to negotiation. Diarising the election deadline on the day of signing is a genuinely high-return habit.`},{id:`funds-sideletters-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`co-investment`,`adverse-selection`],concept:`co-investment`,stem:`How would you test whether a GP is offering its weaker deals as co-investments?`,choices:[`Ask the GP whether it offers its best deals to co-investors`,`Compare the historical performance of co-investments against the main funds over the same periods`,`Assume adverse selection, since GPs always retain their best opportunities`,`Check whether co-investments charge fees`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Every GP will say yes. The question does not discriminate between managers.`,`Correct. The concern is empirical and there is data. A GP that has tracked co-investment performance against fund performance and will share it is answering with evidence rather than assurance.`,`It is not always true — some GPs offer co-investment on their largest and highest-conviction deals precisely because those exceed fund concentration limits. Presuming the answer skips the test.`,`Fee treatment affects the net return and says nothing about deal quality.`],explanation:`There is a plausible mechanism in both directions, which is exactly why it should be tested rather than assumed. That a GP tracks the comparison at all is itself informative about how they think about the conflict.`},{id:`funds-sideletters-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`side-letter`,`liquidity`,`how-it-goes-wrong`],concept:`liquidity-side-letters`,stem:`In an open-ended fund, why is a side letter granting another investor shorter redemption notice more serious than one granting a fee discount?`,choices:[`Because fee discounts are disclosed and liquidity terms are not`,`Because shorter notice lets that investor exit ahead of the gate, funded by sales of the best assets, leaving you with the residue`,`Because liquidity terms affect the fund's regulatory status`,`Because a fee discount to one investor raises fees for everyone else`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Disclosure practice varies for both, and it is not what makes liquidity the more serious issue.`,`Correct. A fee discount costs you nothing directly. Preferential liquidity is a queue position: in stress that investor gets out first at the pre-repricing valuation, and the remaining portfolio is worse for it.`,`Regulatory status is generally unaffected by individual side letters.`,`One investor's discount does not raise another's fee. Fees are set individually.`],explanation:`This is why checking whether your MFN covers liquidity provisions matters so much — many explicitly carve them out, which means the most consequential category of side letter is the one your protection does not reach.`},{id:`funds-sideletters-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`co-investment`,`capability`,`on-the-job`],concept:`on-the-job`,stem:`An investor with a two-person team negotiates hard for co-investment rights instead of a fee discount. Why might that be a poor trade?`,choices:[`Co-investment rights are worth less than fee discounts in all cases`,`Co-investment decisions typically require underwriting a concentrated position in one to three weeks, which a two-person team may not be able to do properly`,`Co-investments cannot be held alongside a fund commitment`,`Fee discounts compound while co-investment returns do not`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Co-investment is often the more valuable provision — for an investor able to use it. The value is conditional on capability, not absolute.`,`Correct. Rights you cannot exercise are worth nothing, and exercising them badly is worse than declining. A fee discount requires no capacity to realise, which for a small team is the better trade.`,`They are routinely held alongside fund commitments; that is the entire arrangement.`,`Both affect returns and neither has a compounding advantage of that kind.`],explanation:`Matching the terms you negotiate to the capability you actually have is a general principle worth carrying: enhanced reporting you cannot analyse and co-investment you cannot underwrite are both decorative, and negotiating capital spent on them is wasted.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
